WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.331
Now let's see how shear flow behaves when booms carry the direct stress.

00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:07.760
Cut out one boom, with a short length d z along the span.

00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:13.411
The skin on one side pulls along the boom with shear flow q one.

00:00:13.411 --> 00:00:16.667
The skin on the other side, with q two.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:26.351
Along the boom, the bending stress changes a little, from sigma z to sigma z plus d sigma z.

00:00:26.351 --> 00:00:29.400
So the boom force changes too.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.473
Add up the forces along the boom.

00:00:32.473 --> 00:00:34.910
The sigma z B r terms cancel.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:43.060
Divide by d z, and you're left with q two minus q one equals minus d sigma z over d z, times B r.

00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:48.398
Bending stress is M x over I x x, times y.

00:00:48.398 --> 00:00:56.607
And the moment changes along the span at the rate of the shear force: d M x by d z is S y.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.439
So d sigma z by d z is S y over I x x, times y.

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:15.360
And the jump at a boom is minus S y over I x x, times B r, times y r.

00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:23.332
Between booms the skin carries no direct stress, so the shear flow can't change there.

00:01:23.332 --> 00:01:26.913
It's constant, and it only jumps when it crosses a boom.

00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:35.073
Here's the channel from your slides: four booms of three hundred square millimeters, two hundred millimeters above and below the axis.

00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:42.088
I x x is four times three hundred times two hundred squared.

00:01:42.088 --> 00:01:45.082
That's forty eight million.

00:01:45.082 --> 00:01:54.953
So S y over I x x is forty eight hundred over forty eight million: ten to the minus four.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.677
Start at the free edge, where the shear flow is zero.

00:01:59.677 --> 00:02:07.357
Crossing boom one: zero, minus ten to the minus four, times three hundred, times two hundred.

00:02:07.357 --> 00:02:10.660
That's minus six newtons per millimeter.

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:11.625
Your turn.

00:02:11.625 --> 00:02:15.113
Pause here and do checkpoint three point one in your guide.

00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:21.320
Boom two adds another minus six.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.213
That's minus twelve in the web.

00:02:24.533 --> 00:02:29.813
Boom three is two hundred below the axis, so its jump is plus six.

00:02:29.813 --> 00:02:31.253
Back to minus six.

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:36.533
And boom four brings it to zero at the free edge, just as it must.

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:41.258
We said the load acts through the shear centre.

00:02:41.258 --> 00:02:42.278
What is that?

00:02:42.278 --> 00:02:48.247
It's the one point where a shear load bends the section without twisting it.

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:51.480
Look at the forces in the walls.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.495
Each flange carries six times two hundred: twelve hundred newtons.

00:02:55.495 --> 00:02:59.814
The web carries twelve times four hundred: forty eight hundred newtons.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.387
The web's force equals the load, good.

00:03:03.387 --> 00:03:18.180
But the two flange forces point opposite ways, so together they make a couple: twelve hundred times four hundred, which is four hundred eighty thousand newton millimeters.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:24.077
For no twist, the load has to balance that couple.

00:03:24.077 --> 00:03:29.979
Forty eight hundred times e equals four hundred eighty thousand.

00:03:29.979 --> 00:03:34.867
So e is one hundred millimeters, outside the channel.

00:03:37.934 --> 00:03:43.096
Notice that the shear centre doesn't have to be inside the material.

00:03:43.096 --> 00:03:49.854
For a wing, we want to know where it is, because lift that misses it will twist the wing.

00:03:50.733 --> 00:03:52.865
A box is a closed section.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:04:00.573
Its walls go all the way around, so there's no free edge where we know the shear flow is zero.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:01.881
So we make one.

00:04:01.881 --> 00:04:03.516
Imagine cutting any wall.

00:04:03.516 --> 00:04:07.700
Now it's an open section, and the shear flow is zero at the cut.

00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:13.471
March around from the cut, jumping at each boom, exactly as for the channel.

00:04:13.471 --> 00:04:16.833
The result is called the basic shear flow, q b.

00:04:17.266 --> 00:04:19.714
Of course, the real box isn't cut.

00:04:19.714 --> 00:04:25.546
Closing the cut adds one extra constant shear flow, q s zero, all the way around.

00:04:25.866 --> 00:04:33.243
To find q s zero, use moments: the shear flows must make the same moment as the load.

00:04:33.243 --> 00:04:41.226
And if the load is at the shear centre, there's a simpler condition: the box must not twist.
